# quickjs: js_parse_skip_parens_token memoised within one parse
#
# VitaBrowser patch 499. An expression starting with '(' (and a few '['
# / '{' cases) is scanned ahead to its matching close to see what follows
# (an arrow, an assignment, a comma); the parser then rewinds and parses
# the tokens for real, and every inner opener later asks the same question
# about its own contents -- nested minified code was tokenised once per
# nesting level. Simulator: ~10% of github.com's page-load main-thread
# time (patch 498 tree) was in this function.
#
# A scan passes over every inner opener, its matching close and the token
# after it, which is all an inner scan computes. Each opener whose close is
# reached cleanly is recorded by its source position (a per-parse
# open-addressing table on JSParseState, js_malloc_rt -- never throws --
# freed when js_parse_program returns) with the bits its own scan would
# report (';' and '...' directly inside, '=' anywhere inside) and the
# token after its close ('of' mapped; line-break flag kept so both
# no_line_terminator modes are answered). A lookup hit returns at once,
# leaving the parse state as the full scan does (it rewinds).
#
# Exact under lexing context: every record keeps the recording function's
# is_strict_mode (strict mode makes legacy octal escapes an error) and is
# reused only under the same; a record whose region or following token
# holds yield, await or a strict-mode reserved word (update_token_ident
# lexes those by context) also keeps the function and its
# in_function_body and is reused only under both. Errors, the end of input
# and the 256-level limit leave no record. VITA_SKIP_CHECK builds also run
# the scan on every hit and count disagreements.
# Applied by tools/fetch-quickjs.sh.

